In response to the latest tool implementation at xFusionCorp Industries, the system admins require the creation of a service user account. Here are the specifics:
Create a user named kirsty in App Server 3 without a home directory.
Solution:
Command:
sudo useradd -M Kirsty
Verification:
id kirsty
ls -ld /home/kirsty
•	id kirsty will show the user exists.
•	/home/kirsty should not exist.

Note:
Important useradd Flags
1.	-m
o	Create the user’s home directory (default /home/username).
o	Example:
o	sudo useradd -m anmol
2.	-M
o	Do not create a home directory.
o	Used for service users (like your task with kirsty).
3.	-s SHELL
o	Specify the login shell.
o	Example:
o	sudo useradd -s /bin/bash anmol
o	sudo useradd -s /sbin/nologin backupuser
4.	-d HOME_DIR
o	Set a custom home directory path.
o	Example:
o	sudo useradd -m -d /opt/devhome devuser
5.	-e YYYY-MM-DD
o	Set an account expiry date.
o	Example:
o	sudo useradd -e 2023-12-31 tempuser
6.	-g GROUP
o	Specify the primary group.
o	Example:
o	sudo useradd -g developers devuser
7.	-G GROUPS
o	Add the user to supplementary (secondary) groups.
o	Example:
o	sudo useradd -G docker,sudo devuser
8.	-u UID
o	Assign a specific user ID instead of auto.
o	Example:
o	sudo useradd -u 1050 customuser
9.	-c COMMENT
o	Add a description (stored in /etc/passwd).
o	Example:
o	sudo useradd -c "Project Developer" devuser
10.	-r
o	Create a system account (UID < 1000 usually).
o	Example:
o	sudo useradd -r serviceuser



